Topic:Evolution of gaming platforms 

The rise of Arcades:
Back in the late 1970s through the mid-1990s arcades were the ultimate hangout spot for kids and teenagers. They were usually tucked inside shopping malls or local corner shops so going to the arcade wasn't just about playing a game it was what you did on the weekend to meet up with friends. You had to physically be there. You'd put your coin on the edge of the machine to call next stand shoulder to shoulder with strangers and crowd around whoever was playing on Street Fighter or Pac Man. Gaming back then was loud, public, and completely tied to your local neighborhood. And when u get bored of the acrade you were able to go around the shopping mall so you and your friends wouldnt get bored. Which is why acrades where such a good thing back then for kids or teenagers.

Console:
Once home consoles took over starting with older systems and leading all the way up to things like the new gen console the PlayStation and Xbox  that whole dynamic moved a lot of gamers to indoors. Suddenly you didnt need to leave the house or spend pocket money at the mall on acrades because the games on your living room TV looked just as good, if not better. Instead of begging a lift into town gaming became a normal part of everyday life at home where you could play for ages without realising its dinner or stay up late with school friends after homework was done. At first this created the couch co-op era where friends would still come over to your house to play split-screen games like Mario Kart or FIFA together on the same sofa. On the couch if someone was being annoying or cheating you couldnt just mute them or leave the lobby—you had to deal with it face to face laugh it off or pass the controller.

However once high speed internet and online networks like PlayStation Network and Xbox Live became standard people stopped needing to be in the same room. On the plus side you could hop on a headset every night after school stay in touch with online friends or your real friends (IRL) who moved away or went to different schools and team up with players from completely different countries. On the downside local hangouts started dying out, and social dynamics became way more anonymous and transactional. Matchmaking put you with random strangers instead of people you knew in real life and hiding behind a screen and a microphone made online trash-talking and toxicity much more common. Over time as more games dropped split screen altogether playing together started looking like four people sitting in four different bedrooms with headsets on instead of crowding around one TV.

Cloud Computing:
Now with cloud gaming services like Xbox Cloud Gaming and GeForce Now gaming is becoming even more accessible and mainstream. Because the heavy computing happens on remote servers you dont even need to buy a €500 console anymore. You can play huge games on a cheap laptop, a tablet, or a phone. Societally, this has opened up gaming to almost everyone, breaking down the old stereotype that video games are just for hardcore nerds. Games like Fortnite have basically became the new shopping malls. Instead of walking around town with your friends, teenagers or kid log into a shared virtual world to chat, mess around, and even watch live virtual concert together.  

 





